<p>President Trump moved to reduce the U.S. military presence in Germany, with the Pentagon announcing the withdrawal of about 5,000 troops and Trump later saying the number would go “a lot further” than that. Pentagon officials said the initial withdrawal would be completed over the next six to 12 months. The United States has more than 36,000 active-duty troops in Germany, part of an 80,000-to-100,000-strong U.S. presence across Europe. The decision followed public tensions between Trump and German Chancellor Friedrich Merz over the U.S.-Israeli war on Iran and European allies’ reluctance to join the conflict, although Merz rejected any direct link between their disagreements and the troop decision. European leaders discussed the announcement at a meeting in Armenia, where officials weighed its effect on NATO planning.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/_ILYZfp4u8Tf4o5QDlls8ZM14NYwfIj5SMuuLN6zMt3NEOEB9Drgm3LJ42CYq6iJonKWgqRKf4F3Y6rRfkKIphOsigECbhsahbD17g8REJUioRabhIA2HRHRLyBBh0S3dRJmVwr7_YgScpXREAS102uRD8yxN7lHM22onEIzeRrIbA8-kYfcW6hrcbLUDtd0LzZCsMN5hKFLf76xV7EGuw' alt='Soldiers at a U.S. training facility in Hohenfels, southern Germany, last month.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Missile plans: Analysts said the reported cancellation or suspension of planned U.S. long-range deployments could have greater strategic effect than the troop drawdown because Germany had expected them as an interim capability while European deep-strike systems were still in development.</li><li>Security impact: German officials and analysts played down the immediate military effect of losing one brigade-sized contingent but said a full U.S. departure would require a more deliberate transition.</li><li>Relocation debate: U.S. lawmakers and European officials discussed whether departing forces should move to NATO’s eastern flank, but reports cited infrastructure limits in countries such as Poland and Romania.</li></ul><blockquote>I wouldn&#x27;t exaggerate that because I think we are expecting that Europe is taking more charge of its own security. - Jonas Gahr Støre</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>German Chancellor Friedrich Merz: Merz said Germany had to accept that Trump does not share his views and keep working with Washington through NATO despite disagreements over Iran. (<a href='https://www.geo.tv/latest/662734-german-chancellor-merz-dismisses-rift-with-trump-rejects-link-to-us-troop-cuts-in-germany'>Geo News</a>)</li><li>German lawmaker Siemtje Moeller: Moeller said the issue was not just troop numbers but trust in long-term partnerships, warning that abrupt decisions made allied planning look increasingly unpredictable. (<a href='https://humenglish.com/latest/trumps-germany-troop-cuts-spark-concern-over-nato-unity/'>Hum English</a>)</li><li>NATO Secretary General Mark Rutte: Rutte said European countries had “got the message” on defense burdens and would provide the logistics and basing support needed inside the alliance. (<a href='https://www.libertaddigital.com/defensa/2026-05-04/rutte-tras-el-anuncio-de-eeuu-de-retirar-5-000-militares-de-alemania-los-paises-europeos-han-captado-el-mensaje-1b-7398750/'>Libertad Digital</a>)</li><li>Romanian President Nicușor Dan: Dan said the move was not a surprise and fit an older U.S. strategic shift toward the Indo-Pacific, adding that Europe had begun compensating for reduced U.S. security support. (<a href='https://www.digi24.ro/stiri/actualitate/politica/nicusor-dan-despre-decizia-sua-de-a-retrage-mii-de-militari-americani-din-germania-nu-e-o-surpriza-3755663'>Digi24</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://abcnews.com/International/wireStory/european-leaders-trumps-troop-drawdown-germany-new-proof-132627308'>As US plans fewer troops in Germany, Europe sees need for bigger role within NATO</a> - abcnews.com</li><li><a href='https://www.csmonitor.com/World/Europe/2026/0504/trump-merz-germany-us-bases-troop-withdrawal'>Trump threatens to pull troops from Germany. Why 